    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini-tabline\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nI love mini plugin. It has greatly simplified my nvim workflow!\r\n\r\nIn the mini tabline, there is no option to disable hidden buffers. Reading the tabline when buffers are closed causes confusion in navigation. Could you please add an option to not show hidden buffers in tabline? A screenshot is attached for reference. \r\nIn the screenshot, there are only two active tabs, the others are hidden buffers. \r\nOne more thing to note is that toggling `set_vim_settings` to true or false doesn't seem to have any effect on this behavior. I've tried both.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11544150/181918338-c6efa57c-0d8d-496a-b715-d41d6e10996b.png)\r\n\r\n\r\n### Neovim version\r\n\r\n0.7.2\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n1. `nvim -nu minimal.lua`\r\n2. Open multiple tabs with different files\r\n3. Close a subset of the tabs by quitting those files/buffers\r\n4. Observe that the closed tabs still show up in tabline even after closing\r\n\r\nMy tabline config: \r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.tabline').setup({\r\n  tabpage_section = 'right',\r\n  set_vim_settings = true,\r\n})\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nbuffers that are closed shouldn't show up in the tabline\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\nclosed buffers never goes away from tabline causing confusion in navigation",
