            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> But there are only two files I am working with\r\n\r\nNot really. In (Neo)Vim buffer, window, and tab page are different. As [windows-intro](https://vimhelp.org/windows.txt.html#windows-intro) has it:\r\n```\r\nA buffer is the in-memory text of a file.\r\nA window is a viewport on a buffer.\r\nA tab page is a collection of windows.\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn your example:\r\n- `:e test1.txt` creates a buffer for file `test1.txt` and shows it in current window.\r\n- `:tabnew test2.txt` creates a buffer for file `test2.txt`, creates new tab page, and shows this buffer in new window.\r\n- `:tabnew test3.txt` does the same, but for `test3.txt`.\r\n- `:q!` closes window from tag page 3 (which leads to closing of a tab page itself). However, buffer is still there. It is hidden (because it is not shown anywhere), but you can still use it (try `:bnext` after last command). 'mini.tabline' is intended to show buffer, not tab pages or windows.\r\n\r\nYou can get more information about this by searching something like \"buffer vs tabs in vim\".",
