            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> As [windows-intro](https://vimhelp.org/windows.txt.html#windows-intro) has it:\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> A buffer is the in-memory text of a file.\r\n> A window is a viewport on a buffer.\r\n> A tab page is a collection of windows.\r\n> ```\r\n\r\nYou don't *close* buffer with `:q`, you *close* window which displayed that buffer. The 'mini.tabline' module is designed as \"tabline showing listed buffers\", so it is reasonable that it still shows buffer which is still there. To actually \"close buffer\" there are [`:bdelete`](https://neovim.io/doc/user/windows.html#%3Abdelete) and [`:bwipeout`](https://neovim.io/doc/user/windows.html#%3Abwipeout).\r\n\r\nTo show only tabpages with some of their extra information in tabline, use default tabline: set `showtabline` option to 2 and don't set up 'mini.tabline'.",
